## Proof

Let $X$ be a CW-complex, $e$ a $q$-cell of $X$ (the case $q=0$ is trivial, so assume $q \geq 1$), and let
$$\chi_1, \chi_2 : (D^q, S^{q-1}) \to (X^q, X^{q-1})$$
be two characteristic maps for $e$. By definition each $\chi_i$ maps $\operatorname{int}(D^q)$ homeomorphically onto $e$ and maps $S^{q-1}$ into $X^{q-1}$.

---

**Step 1.  The target group and the quotient picture.**

The cellular chain group is $C_q(X) = H_q(X^q, X^{q-1})$.  Collapsing $X^{q-1}$ to a point gives a wedge of $q$-spheres
$$X^q / X^{q-1} \;\cong\; \bigvee_{e'} S^{q}_{e'},$$
one sphere for each $q$-cell $e'$, and $H_q(X^q, X^{q-1}) \cong \bigoplus_{e'} \mathbb{Z}\langle e'\rangle$.

Each characteristic map $\chi_i$ descends (since $\chi_i(S^{q-1}) \subseteq X^{q-1}$) to a based map
$$\bar\chi_i : S^q \;=\; D^q / S^{q-1} \;\longrightarrow\; \bigvee_{e'} S^{q}_{e'},$$
and $H_q(\chi_i)[D^q] = H_q(\bar\chi_i)[S^q]$.

---

**Step 2.  Only the $e$-summand is hit.**

Since $\chi_i(D^q) \subseteq e \cup X^{q-1}$, after collapsing $X^{q-1}$ the image of $\bar\chi_i$ lies in $S^q_e \cup \{\text{wedge point}\} = S^q_e$.  Hence for every $e' \neq e$ the composition $\pi_{e'} \circ \bar\chi_i : S^q \to S^q_{e'}$ (projection to the $e'$-summand followed by collapse) is constant, so
$$\deg(\pi_{e'} \circ \bar\chi_i) = 0, \qquad e' \neq e.$$
Thus $H_q(\chi_i)[D^q]$ is supported on the single summand $\mathbb{Z}\langle e\rangle$.

---

**Step 3.  The coefficient on the $e$-summand is $\pm 1$.**

Let $p \in S^q$ be the point corresponding to the collapsed $S^{q-1} \subset D^q$, and let $w \in \bigvee S^q_{e'}$ be the wedge point.  Because $\chi_i$ restricts to a homeomorphism $\operatorname{int}(D^q) \xrightarrow{\;\sim\;} e$, the map
$$\pi_e \circ \bar\chi_i : S^q \setminus \{p\} \;\longrightarrow\; S^q_e \setminus \{w\}$$
is a homeomorphism.  Therefore $\pi_e \circ \bar\chi_i : S^q \to S^q_e$ is a continuous map that is a homeomorphism on the complement of a single point.

Such a map has degree $\pm 1$.  Indeed, pick any regular value $y \in S^q_e \setminus \{w\}$; it has exactly one preimage $x \in S^q \setminus \{p\}$, and the local degree at $x$ is $+1$ or $-1$ (orientation preserved or reversed by the local homeomorphism).  Since $S^q \setminus \{p\}$ is connected for $q \geq 1$, this local degree is constant over all of $S^q \setminus \{p\}$, so
$$\deg(\pi_e \circ \bar\chi_i) = \pm 1.$$

Consequently
$$H_q(\chi_i)[D^q] \;=\; \varepsilon_i\,\langle e\rangle, \qquad \varepsilon_i \in \{+1, -1\}.$$

---

**Step 4.  Conclusion.**

Both $H_q(\chi_1)[D^q]$ and $H_q(\chi_2)[D^q]$ are supported on the same summand $\mathbb{Z}\langle e\rangle$ and each is $\pm\langle e\rangle$.  Hence
$$H_q(\chi_1)[D^q] \;=\; \frac{\varepsilon_1}{\varepsilon_2}\, H_q(\chi_2)[D^q] \;=\; \pm\, H_q(\chi_2)[D^q]. \qquad \blacksquare$$

---

**Remark on the source of the sign.**  The sign $\varepsilon_i = \pm 1$ records whether the homeomorphism $\operatorname{int}(D^q) \to e$ determined by $\chi_i$ preserves or reverses the local orientation.  Two different characteristic maps may differ by an orientation-reversing self-homeomorphism of $D^q$ (pre-composition), which is exactly why only the sign—and not the magnitude—can change.  This is what makes the cellular boundary map well defined up to the choice of orientation of each cell, and is the reason the cellular chain complex is defined only up to a simultaneous sign choice on each generator.

